<TopicPage slug="subjects/sociology/foundational/society-socialisation">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an early lesson in Sociology. You need nothing from earlier lessons — you have
    been doing the subject's homework your whole life without calling it that: living in a
    family, joining queues, learning what gets laughed at and what gets you told off. This
    lesson makes that background knowledge explicit and gives it names.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>state sociology's founding claim — that society has patterns, rates and causes that can be studied the way nature is — and explain what a <strong>social fact</strong> is;</li>
    <li>distinguish primary, secondary, anticipatory socialisation and resocialisation, and name the agents that carry each;</li>
    <li>retell the three classic accounts of how a self forms: the looking-glass self, Mead's role-taking ladder, and Goffman's theatre;</li>
    <li>compare informal and formal social control, and the functionalist and Marxist arguments about what socialisation is <em>for</em>;</li>
    <li>explain how class, gender and ethnicity shape identity together, and what intersectionality adds;</li>
    <li>define role conflict and role strain and recognise both in your own week;</li>
    <li>interrogate any claim that "studies show" something — by checking the method, the sample, and the practical–ethical–theoretical trade-offs.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit: whenever a claim about people appears — including on this page — we ask how
    anyone could know it. "Everyone knows" is where sociology starts, not where it ends.
  </p>

  <h2>The puzzle that starts sociology: how is a society possible?</h2>
  <p>
    Watch a busy street corner for five minutes. Strangers queue without being told, cars
    take turns, pedestrians flow around each other, nobody is directing any of it. Or start
    closer to home: you did not choose your language, your manners, your ambitions or your
    sense of what is normal — you were <strong>socialised</strong> into them. Socialisation
    is the lifelong process by which a society teaches its members how to belong to it.
    Sociology studies how that teaching happens and what it builds.
  </p>
  <p>
    The building blocks need names, because the rest of the lesson runs on them.
    <strong>Norms</strong> are the unwritten rules — queue here, don't sing on the bus. Think
    of them as an operating system nobody sat you down to install. <strong>Roles</strong> are
    the positions you hold — student, sibling, employee — each with a script of expectations
    attached. <strong>Groups</strong> are sets of people who interact and feel they belong
    together. <strong>Institutions</strong> — family, school, religion, media, the state —
    are society's durable structures for passing norms and roles on. A <strong>society</strong>
    itself can be defined as a very large group of people organised into institutions and
    held together over time by formalised relationships; <strong>culture</strong> — the
    knowledge, beliefs and practices a group shares — is the looser, informal side of the
    same thing.
  </p>
  <p>
    The discipline's founding wager was made in 1838, when Auguste Comte coined the name
    "sociology" (from his earlier phrase "social physics"): the claim that society can be
    studied like nature, with patterns, rates and causes, rather than merely judged or
    moralised about. It was a bold claim. People seem gloriously unpredictable.
  </p>
  <p>
    Émile Durkheim's <em>Suicide</em> (1897) made the founding demonstration. Choose what
    looks like the most private decision a person can make — whether to end their own life.
    If that decision were purely individual, the number of suicides in a country would drift
    randomly. It does not: the <em>rate</em> stays stable year after year, and it differs
    systematically between groups. Something beyond any one individual is doing the
    explaining. Durkheim called such patterns <strong>social facts</strong>: ways of acting
    that are external to each of us and yet constrain us. Individuals come and go; the
    pattern persists. That is sociology's object of study.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "sociology just states the obvious — everyone already
  knows people copy each other." Findings sound obvious <em>after</em> you have heard them
  (psychologists call this hindsight bias), but the actual discoveries were surprises:
  nobody expected suicide to have stable social rates, or strangers to conform to a plainly
  wrong answer just because a group said it. And common sense contradicts itself freely —
  "opposites attract" and "birds of a feather flock together" are both folk wisdom. Which is
  right? Only evidence settles it, and that is the whole point of the subject.</p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the founders and the famous experiments — what sociology learned about pulling people around" widget="timeline" config={socTimeline} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the timeline spans 130 years but tells one story — a
    vague intuition being replaced by a measurement each time. Comte supplies the wager;
    Durkheim delivers the proof that rates can be social; then the experiments put numbers
    on group pull itself: about three-quarters of Asch's participants went along with an
    obviously wrong majority at least once, roughly two-thirds of Milgram's obeyed to the
    maximum "shock", and Tajfel's coin-toss strangers favoured their own made-up group
    within minutes. None of these effects is obvious in advance; all of them are measurable.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> society shapes us through norms, roles and institutions, and
    its fingerprints show up in measurable rates. Now we look at the mechanism itself — how
    the teaching actually happens.
  </p>

  <h2>How we learn our identity: four kinds of socialisation</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Primary socialisation</strong> happens in the family in early childhood, and it
    installs the deepest defaults: language (the accent and dialect that will introduce you
    to strangers for life), attachment, first norms, gendered expectations (which toys,
    which chores, which emotions are "for you"), and the class-inflected habits and tastes
    absorbed at home. Nothing later runs as deep, because nothing later arrives before your
    defences do.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Secondary socialisation</strong> takes over as the institutions around you
    widen. School teaches the formal curriculum — and, running silently beneath it, the
    <strong>hidden curriculum</strong>: punctuality, queueing for rewards, obedience to
    systems. Bowles and Gintis argued that the correspondence between school hierarchy and
    workplace hierarchy is the point, not a by-product. The peer group dominates
    adolescence — the one stage of life where you outrank your parents — and belonging is
    traded for conformity. Media socialises at a scale no family can match: role models,
    representations, and now algorithmic feeds in which a private message can go viral and
    become a mass one. Religion, or its secular equivalent, carries values, rituals and
    community — and supplies many of the norms that bite hardest.
  </p>
  <p>
    The last two kinds bracket adult life. <strong>Anticipatory socialisation</strong> is
    the rehearsal: a teenager practising interview answers, or saving photos of a dream
    flat, is learning a role before holding it. <strong>Resocialisation</strong> is the
    forced rewrite: army boot camps, prisons, migration to a country with new rules.
    Erving Goffman called institutions that strip an old identity and rebuild a new one
    <em>total institutions</em> — places where sleep, eating and work all happen under one
    authority. Boot camp is the honest demonstration that identity can be reinstalled by
    design.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="the agents of socialisation — every arrow installs part of you; click each one" widget="diagram" config={agentsCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> click the family node first — it is the primary stage,
    the arrow that arrives before all others. Then click through school, peers, media,
    religion and workplace and watch each one install a different layer. The centre node is
    the crucial one: your identity is the result of every arrow, and yet — unlike a puppet —
    it is also an editor, because people resist and remix every script they receive.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> socialisation is not one event but a sequence of installations
    — family first, institutions second, rehearsals and rewrites after. But how does
    teaching from outside become a self inside?
  </p>

  <h2>The self, three classic accounts</h2>
  <p>
    Charles Horton Cooley's <strong>looking-glass self</strong> (1902) answers with three
    steps, all made of imagination: we imagine how we appear to other people; we imagine
    their judgement of that appearance; and we develop a feeling about ourselves — pride or
    shame — as a result. The mirror is not real glass; it is our guess of others' view. That
    is why a teacher's casual comment can redirect a whole life: the child looks into the
    imagined mirror daily, and the reflection hardens.
  </p>
  <p>
    George Herbert Mead added the developmental ladder. Children first <em>imitate</em>
    others without understanding, then <em>play</em> one role at a time (being the doctor,
    being the parent), and finally grasp the <strong>generalized other</strong> — society's
    expectations as a whole — when they learn to play a game, which requires holding every
    position in mind at once. Taking the role of the other is how the social "me" forms
    around the spontaneous "I".
  </p>
  <p>
    Goffman's <em>The Presentation of Self in Everyday Life</em> (1959) made the whole thing
    theatre. On the <em>front stage</em> — an interview, a classroom, a customer-service
    voice — you perform a managed version of yourself; on the <em>back stage</em> — home,
    the staff room, the phone put down — you drop it. <em>Impression management</em> runs
    continuously, and most of the time nobody notices, which is exactly the sign that the
    performance has been rehearsed to perfection.
  </p>

  <h2>How society controls us</h2>
  <p>
    Control runs on two channels. <strong>Informal control</strong> is the everyday machine:
    smiles and praise are positive <strong>sanctions</strong> (rewards for following norms),
    sneers, gossip and exclusion are negative ones. Its deep version is shame and
    conscience, and it works because norms get <em>internalised</em> — installed so early
    that by adulthood most policing is self-policing. You stop at an empty red light at
    3 a.m. The audience in your head is watching.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Formal control</strong> is the state channel: laws, police, courts, graded
    punishments. It exists because informal control fails at scale — a city of a million
    people cannot run on gossip. Both channels do the same job: keep behaviour inside the
    lines the norms drew.
  </p>
  <p>
    The theoretical argument is over what socialisation is <em>for</em>. Functionalists,
    following Durkheim, see it as society's adhesive: shared norms produce solidarity, and
    even deviance is useful, because punishment is the boundary being publicly re-drawn —
    the scandal clarifies where the line is. Marxists see adhesive with a direction: the
    norms transmitted are the ones that reproduce the existing class order, and the order
    calls the result meritocracy. Both agree on the fact of socialisation; they argue about
    the invoice.
  </p>
  <p>
    Deviance itself got an upgrade worth knowing. Robert Merton's <strong>strain
    theory</strong> starts from a gap: society sells everyone the same goals (success,
    wealth) but distributes the legitimate means unevenly — so some people innovate (crime),
    some ritualistically keep going through the motions, some rebel against the goals
    themselves. Howard Becker's <strong>labelling theory</strong> relocates part of deviance
    in the reaction: call a child a troublemaker often enough and the label becomes their
    <strong>master status</strong>, the one everyone sees first, and the self hardens to fit
    it — a self-fulfilling prophecy. Rosenthal and Jacobson demonstrated the mechanism in
    1968: teachers told that randomly selected pupils were about to "bloom" produced
    measurable IQ gains in exactly those pupils by year's end. The label helped cause the
    behaviour it described.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "rule-breakers are a different kind of person — that is
  why they offend." It feels true because prison populations look unlike us from a
  distance. But Merton's point is that strain can push <em>anyone</em> when goals and means
  diverge, and labelling's point is that a "deviant identity" is partly manufactured by the
  reaction to a first act. Most people break rules at some point; whether the act becomes
  an identity depends on who saw it, and what they did next.</p>

  <h2>What shapes identity: class, gender and ethnicity — together</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Social class</strong> is a person's economic and social standing, and it works
    on identity through what Pierre Bourdieu called the <em>habitus</em>: the tastes, speech
    and bodily manner absorbed at home that schools quietly reward and employers quietly
    read — the <strong>cultural capital</strong> of knowing the right codes. The
    sociolinguist Basil Bernstein made the mechanism concrete with "restricted" and
    "elaborated" language codes. One distinction sharpens the idea: class can change with
    income and education, but a <strong>caste</strong> is a rank fixed by birth. Studying
    the 1930s American South, the sociologists Allison Davis and John Dollard showed race
    operating as caste — Black professionals held a rank their wealth could not buy out of —
    an early demonstration that hierarchy can be inherited in more ways than one.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Gender</strong> is socialised from the first pink or blue item. Ann Oakley
    documented how toys, chores and praise split by sex long before children can put it
    into words. The modern formulation — "doing gender", from West and Zimmerman — treats
    gender not as a possession but as a performance renewed daily: something you
    <em>do</em>, in front of an audience that keeps checking.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Ethnicity</strong> shapes identity through shared ancestry, heritage and
    community — and, unavoidably, through other people's reactions. The psychologist Jean
    Phinney described ethnic identity as developing through stages: first <em>unexamined</em>
    (taken for granted or barely thought about), then an often-triggered <em>search</em> —
    an experience of prejudice or a moment of pride can start it — and finally an
    <em>achieved</em> identity, confidently held. The process is asymmetric: teenagers from
    minority backgrounds generally have to learn the dominant culture's customs as well as
    their own, while the reverse is rarely required — which is where the daily labour of
    code-switching comes from.
  </p>
  <p>
    Everyone is all of these at once, which is why Kimberlé Crenshaw's
    <strong>intersectionality</strong> (1989) supplies the summing rule: a working-class
    teenage girl from a migrant family experiences neither class nor gender nor ethnicity
    separately but as one compounded position — which is why single-axis explanations keep
    missing people. And identity, finally, is negotiated rather than installed: people
    resist, remix and renegotiate every script, which is precisely why socialisation has to
    keep running all our lives.
  </p>

  <h2>Roles, and what happens when they collide</h2>
  <p>
    The role concept deserves its own section because its complications are where real
    lives get lived. Holding many roles at once — student, employee, teammate, daughter or
    son, carer of a grandparent — works smoothly until the scripts conflict. The clash
    between <em>different</em> roles is <strong>role conflict</strong>: the exam revision
    that collides with the shift you promised to cover. The strain <em>within one</em>
    over-stuffed role is <strong>role strain</strong>: a student role that demands study,
    wellbeing, sociability and self-marketing simultaneously is the modern textbook case.
  </p>
  <p>
    People manage these the way Goffman predicted: by scheduling, by quarantining audiences
    ("my work friends never meet my school friends"), by prioritising, and by quietly
    renegotiating the scripts. The <strong>life course</strong> adds the time dimension:
    roles arrive and retire on a schedule society sets — school-leaving, working age,
    retirement — and who moves through that sequence smoothly, and who is derailed by
    caring duties, unemployment or ill health, is itself sharply patterned by class and
    gender. The micro world of roles connects straight back to the macro structure
    sociology exists to describe.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "socialisation is for children — adults are finished
  products." Childhood gets the attention because primary socialisation is the deepest
  layer, but the process never stops: the workplace retrains you in professional norms,
  parenthood hands you a role no course prepares you for, migration forces a rewrite, and
  retirement retires a role that had organised your week for decades. An adult is not a
  finished product; they are a product still under revision.</p>

  <h2>Studying society: how sociologists actually know</h2>
  <p>
    Sociology's claims are only as good as its methods, and the toolkit is worth knowing
    before trusting anyone's — including this page's. <strong>Primary data</strong> is
    collected fresh. <em>Questionnaires</em> reach large numbers cheaply but flatten answers
    into boxes; <em>interviews</em>, structured or open, get depth at the cost of scale;
    <em>participant observation</em> — the researcher joining the group, as sociologists
    have joined gangs, factories and football crowds — yields unmatched understanding that
    it would be dishonest to generalise from. <strong>Secondary data</strong> reuses
    existing material: official statistics (free, large, and collected for someone else's
    purposes — which is exactly the caveat), historical documents, and other researchers'
    studies.
  </p>
  <p>
    Sampling decides everything in between. A <em>representative</em> sample lets a
    thousand people speak for a million; a convenience sample — whoever was nearby — makes
    a thousand people speak only for themselves. And every project runs the
    <strong>PET</strong> gauntlet: <em>Practical</em> (time, money, access), <em>Ethical</em>
    (informed consent, confidentiality, harm — the lesson Milgram taught the whole field),
    <em>Theoretical</em> (does the method actually capture what it claims to?). When you
    read "studies show", these are the questions in the sieve.
  </p>
  <p>
    The methods pair with the theory in a way that explains half the arguments in the
    subject. Positivists, following Durkheim, favour large-scale quantitative data because
    they want sociology to find laws the way physics does. Interpretivists favour
    qualitative depth because they doubt society has laws at all — only meanings, which
    must be understood from inside (the German contrast is <em>Erklären</em>, explaining,
    versus <em>Verstehen</em>, understanding). Ask "is crime rising?" and the positivist
    reaches for the recorded-crime series; the interpretivist points out that "crime" is
    whatever gets reported, recorded and defined, so one reality can produce two opposite
    series. Both are right about their half, which is why strong studies triangulate —
    using each method's strength to cover the other's blind spot.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A toddler begins saying "thank you" unprompted. Name the process, the agent and the kind of socialisation involved.</li>
    <li>Distinguish role conflict from role strain, with one example of each.</li>
    <li>Why did Durkheim study suicide <em>rates</em> rather than individual suicides, and what does the answer make the rate an example of?</li>
    <li>A headline announces "crime is rising". Give one question a positivist would ask and one an interpretivist would ask before believing it.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Socialisation, carried out by the family, and of the
    <em>primary</em> kind — the deepest defaults are installed in early childhood. (2) Role
    conflict is a clash <em>between</em> two roles (revision versus the promised shift); role
    strain is tension <em>inside</em> one role (the student role demanding study, wellbeing
    and sociability all at once). If you swapped them, ask: how many roles are involved? (3)
    Because rates are stable and patterned while individual motives are not — stability plus
    group differences point to causes beyond the individual, which makes the rate a social
    fact. (4) Positivist: is the measurement consistent year to year — recorded crime, same
    definitions? Interpretivist: what counts as "crime" here — who reports, who records,
    who defines? Neither question alone settles the headline.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Sociology's founding claim: society has patterns, rates and causes that can be studied like nature — and <strong>social facts</strong> persist while individuals come and go.</li>
    <li>Socialisation is lifelong and comes in four kinds: primary (family), secondary (schools, peers, media, religion), anticipatory (rehearsal) and resocialisation (rewrite).</li>
    <li>The self forms socially: Cooley's looking-glass self, Mead's role-taking ladder to the generalized other, Goffman's front-stage performance.</li>
    <li>Control runs on informal sanctions and internalised conscience, backed by formal law; functionalists call the result solidarity, Marxists call it reproduction.</li>
    <li>Deviance is partly in the reaction: strain theory explains the push, labelling theory explains the identity, and labels can cause what they describe.</li>
    <li>Class, gender and ethnicity shape identity together — intersectionality is the summing rule, not an optional extra.</li>
    <li>Trust a study only after the sieve: method, sample, and the practical–ethical–theoretical trade-offs behind it.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    Identity is the thread that ties this subject together, and the coming lessons pull it
    tighter: race, ethnicity and identity, and gender and society, take the lenses from this
    page and give each a full treatment. Social networks shows what happens when the
    "audience" in the looking-glass becomes a literal, mappable web of contacts, and the
    social-theory lesson takes you from Marx to Bourdieu — the deep architecture behind
    functionalism, Marxism and cultural capital. The methods section here is your passport
    to all of them: in every later lesson, the same sieve applies.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Durkheim's <em>Suicide</em> repays a second look because it is the discipline's
    methodological charter. He had no interest in individual motives; he aggregated European
    death records and found rates stable for years and patterned by religion (Protestants
    higher than Catholics — he argued the difference was social integration), by marriage,
    by social crisis. His two types — <em>egoistic</em> (too little integration) and
    <em>anomic</em> (norms collapsed by sudden upheaval) — turned a private tragedy into an
    index of social health, and the move still powers every study that finds social patterns
    in individual outcomes.
  </p>
  <p>
    The experiments have their own afterlife. Milgram's obedience findings replicated
    broadly — Jerry Burger's 2009 rerun, run under far tighter ethics, found obedience rates
    barely changed — but the deception-and-distress backlash they triggered built the
    modern research-ethics apparatus: a case where sociology's methods were disciplined by
    sociology's own findings. The newest chapter is algorithmic. Feeds now select which
    versions of everyone we see, recommendation engines are socialisers that update a
    million times a day, and the century-old insight that a society exists in its
    communication — John Dewey's phrase, from 1916 — has acquired a literal meaning he
    never quite imagined: whether the looking-glass self still describes a mirror, or a
    hall of curated mirrors, is the question this subfield is currently writing.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Durkheim, <em>Suicide</em> (1897); Cooley (1902); Mead, <em>Mind, Self and Society</em> (1934); Asch (1951); Milgram (1963); Goffman, <em>The Presentation of Self in Everyday Life</em> (1959); Tajfel & Turner (1979); Bourdieu, <em>Distinction</em> (1984); Rosenthal & Jacobson (1968); Burger (2009). Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>
